          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What actually happens in the airways during an asthma flare-up?",
          "answer": "During an asthma flare-up, the airways undergo significant physiological changes characterized by inflammation and constriction.\n\n1. **Airway Inflammation**: The lining of the airways becomes inflamed. This inflammation can be due to various triggers, leading to increased mucus production and swelling in the airway passages.\n\n2. **Bronchoconstriction**: The muscles surrounding the airways may constrict, which narrows the airways and makes it difficult for air to pass through. This results in airflow obstruction. \n\n3. **Mucus Production**: Excess mucus in the airways further obstructs airflow, contributing to symptoms such as coughing and wheezing. \n\nThese changes lead to the hallmark symptoms of asthma, including wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness, and coughing, which can vary in intensity over time (source: \"Asthma,\" General clinical reference). \n\nIf you need more specific information about asthma management or related topics, feel free to ask!\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
